Title:  Revealed Ambition and the Transition through Education
Abstract:  Howdemanding a qualification should students choose relative to their academic ability?
We argue that this choice reveals ambition and study howthe choice relates to later outcomes.
Linking PISA2012 data for Switzerland to administrative records,we followstudents for more
than ten years. We measure revealed ambition as the difference between the median PISA
score of entrants to a student’s chosen upper secondary qualification and the student’s own
score. Qualifications encompass more than 200 vocational occupations and several general
education programmes with widely varying academic requirements. Consistent with our
interpretation, revealed ambition is associated, conditional on ability, with socioeconomic
background and, strongly, with the occupational status that students expect to hold at age 30.
The results reveal a trade-off: ambitious students are less likely to complete upper secondary
education on time, but more likely to obtain a tertiary degree. Among vocational students,
they also earn more.
